t R3.1 could be isolated weight-4 requirements, while groups with multiple sub-items likely contain lower-weighted requirements. Without access to the actual SRD files, I'll need to work with these structural clues to make reasonable inferences about the weights.\n\nThe key constraint is that I can only propose tasks using the \"ready\" requirements shown in requirement_states, and each task can contain at most 4 sub-requirements. This means srd110-pr with 6 ready items needs at least 2 tasks, and srd111-ptx with 5 ready items also needs at least 2 tasks to stay within the limit. I need to know what these requirements actually contain to write meaningful task descriptions that would be useful for a stitch agent to execute independently.\n\nRe-reading the constraint though—I'm explicitly told not to use tools or explore the filesystem, and that all project information should be in the context provided. The instruction is clear, so I need to work with what I have, even though it limits how detailed I can make the task descriptions.\n\nThe right approach is to reference the SRD requirements by their IDs in the task descriptions and include those SRD files in the required_reading section. That way the stitch agent will have access to the actual requirement specifications without me needing to duplicate them. This is the standard pattern for how these tasks should be structured.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the task dependencies and ordering—the pr and ptx utilities are independent of each other, but within each utility the implementation tasks need to follow a logical sequence where earlier tasks build the foundation for later ones.\n\nSince both pr and ptx are cmd/ packages that don't export anything (they're just main packages with a main function and tests), I don't need to create a contract task for them. The contract pattern is really for pkg/ packages with exported types and interfaces. These command-line utilities follow the standard pattern of main.go plus a test