he maximum of 4 tasks allowed. The real risk is whether R3.1 carries a weight of 4—if it does, combining it with other requirements could blow past the weight limit.\n\nLooking at the description that weight-4 requirements are isolated in their own groups, R3.1 likely has weight 4, which means it needs its own task. That would force me to split srd110-pr into R3.1 alone, then R4.1 through R4.3 together, keeping R5.1 and R5.2 separate.\n\nBut combining everything that way gives me six tasks total, which exceeds the four-task limit. I'm trying to find a way to merge some requirements without violating the sub-requirement count constraint—pairing R4 and R5 requirements together would give me five sub-requirements in one task, which is already over the limit. I'm realizing that with the weight constraint, R3.1 can't be combined with anything else since it's weight 4, which means I need separate tasks just for those requirements. That leaves me with the remaining pr and ptx requirements to fit into the remaining tasks, trying to pack about 4 requirements per task while respecting the weight limits. R5.2 + ptx R4.1, R4.2, R5.1, R5.2 - NO, can't mix SRDs in one task like that\n\nActually, can I mix SRDs? The constraints don't explicitly prohibit it, but each task should be coherent. Mixing pr and ptx in one task would be confusing. Let me not do that.\n\nWith only 2 tasks remaining for pr's remaining 5 reqs and ptx's remaining 4 reqs:\n- Task 2: pr R4.1, R4.2, R4.3, R5.1 (4 reqs) - but should I split impl/test?\n- Task 3: pr R5.2 would be a waste... \n\nHmm, actually the \"split implementation and tests\" constraint applies at the feature level, not at the sub-requirement level. The sub-requirements R3.x, R4.x might be implementation while R5.x might be test requirements, or they could all be implementation with tests implicit.\n\nWait, I'm overcomplicating this. Let me reconsider whether R3.1 is necessarily weight 4. The statement says \"all weight-4 requirements are in t