tests depending on ptx implementation.\n\nNow I'm checking whether each task fits within its weight budget of 4 units. Task 0 has four requirements totaling 4 units, task 1 has two requirements totaling 2 units, task 2 has three requirements totaling 3 units, and task 3 has two requirements totaling 2 units—all within their limits.\n\nBut I need to reconsider if any individual requirement carries a weight of 4, since that would force it into its own task. If R3.1 is actually weight 4, then grouping it with R4.1, R4.2, and R4.3 would exceed the budget. I should isolate heavier requirements and redistribute the others accordingly.\n\nLooking back at the project description, it says weight-4 requirements are in their own groups within the SRD document. Since requirement_states shows R3.1 as a sub-requirement, I'm wondering if R3.1 itself is weight 4, which would explain why it's isolated in group R3.\n\nFor groups R4 and R5 with multiple sub-requirements ready, those are likely weight 1-2 each. If R3.1 is indeed weight 4, I can't pair it with anything else, but that leaves me struggling with the 4-task limit to cover everything. Let me restructure: Task 0 gets R3.1 alone if it's weight 4, then Task 1 combines R4.1, R4.2, R4.3, and R5.1 as individual weight-1 requirements.\n\nBut I'm running into the same problem—mixing implementation and test requirements violates the split rule. The same issue appears with srd111-ptx when I try Task 2 with R3.1 alone and Task 3 with R4 and R5 combined. This leaves srd110-pr R5.2 unassigned, and if I try to fit everything into five requirements in one task, I exceed the 4 sub-requirement limit. None of these approaches work.\n\nLet me reconsider the constraint structure. With 11 total requirements, a 4-requirement maximum per task, and 4 tasks available, I have 16 slots total—enough capacity. The real bottleneck is that R3.1 items are weight 4 and must stand alone, consuming 2 tasks just for those. That leaves 2 tasks for the remaining