at if R3.1 isn't weight 4 after all? I could still isolate it in its own task to be cautious, then pack the remaining capacity with R4.x and R5.x requirements. This way I cover 10 of 11 requirements across four tasks without risking a weight violation.\n\nNow I'm checking whether mixing requirement groups from the same SRD is allowed—the constraints don't seem to prohibit it, so Task 1 can hold both R4 and R5 items from srd110-pr. I could also expand Task 3 to include the final R5.2 requirement, giving me complete coverage across all four tasks.\n\nLet me reconsider the structure: Task 0 stays at one requirement, Task 1 takes the three R4 items from srd110-pr, and Task 2 handles srd111-ptx R3.1. But mixing srd110-pr and srd111-ptx in the same task would violate the constraint, so I need to keep them separate.\n\nGoing with the cleaner approach: four tasks covering nine requirements total, with R5.1 and R5.2 deferred for both SRDs. The main uncertainty is whether R3.1's weight allows me to safely fit this within capacity, but this ordering feels right.\n\nNow I'm drafting the full YAML structure. Each task needs an index, title with the requirement IDs included, dependency references, and a detailed description covering deliverable type, required reading, affected files, which requirements it addresses, design decisions, and acceptance criteria.\n\nFor the design decisions, I can only include what's derived from the architecture since I don't have access to the SRD itself—so that's the SIGPIPE handler installation and following the existing cmd/ package patterns. The acceptance criteria will focus on build success, linting, and whether the specific requirements are actually implemented.\n\nNow I need to write the task descriptions in a way that clearly tells the agent what to build and implement.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":43283,"cache_read_input_tokens":11845,"